The single was released in the United States in February 1980. It spent six consecutive weeks at number one on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] and was certified Gold (for one million copies sold) by the [[Recording Industry Association of America|RIAA]]. It also spent four weeks at No. 2 on the US [[Dance Club Songs|dance chart]]. The single was also number 1 on ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' magazine's 1980 year-end chart. The song lists at No. 57 on ''Billboard's'' All Time Top 100.<ref>{{cite magazine |url= http://www.billboard.com/bbcom/specials/hot100/charts/top100-titles-50.shtml |title= Billboard Hot 100 Chart 50th Anniversary |magazine= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |access-date= August 1, 2018 |archive-date= May 25, 2012 |archive-url= https://archive.today/20120525174923/http://www.billboard.com/bbcom/specials/hot100/charts/top100-titles-50.shtml |url-status= dead }}</ref> It was released in the UK two months later, where it became Blondie's fourth UK No. 1 single on the [[UK singles chart]] in little over a year. The song was also played on a [[BT Group|British Telecom]] advert in the 1980s.{{cn|date=June 2023}}

''[[Record World]]'' called "Call Me" a "stirring [[Electronic dance music|electronic dance]] cut".<ref name="rw">{{cite magazine |date=February 16, 1980 |title=Hits of the Week |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Record-World/80s/80/RW-1980-02-16.pdf |magazine=Record World |page=1 |accessdate=2023-02-16}}</ref> Writing for ''[[Stereogum]]'', Tom Breihan graded the song a 10/10, saying it was a "sleek and efficient [...] mechanistic marvel" that "whips you along into the sleazy neon future".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Breihan |first=Tom |date=2020-03-23 |title=The Number Ones: Blondie's "Call Me" |url=https://www.stereogum.com/2077317/the-number-ones-blondies-call-me/columns/the-number-ones/ |access-date=2025-01-07 |website=Stereogum |language=en}}</ref> In 1981, the song was nominated for a [[Grammy Award for Best Rock Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal]] at the [[23rd Annual Grammy Awards]], as well as for a [[Golden Globe Award for Best Original Song|Golden Globe for Best Original Song]] at the [[38th Golden Globes]].{{cn|date=June 2023}}

Twenty-four years after its original release, "Call Me" was ranked at number 289 on the [[list of Rolling Stone's 500 Greatest Songs of All Time|list of ''Rolling Stone'''s 500 Greatest Songs of All Time]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=December 11, 2003 |title=500 Greatest Songs of All Time |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/500-greatest-songs-of-all-time-151127/blondie-call-me-53061/ |access-date=October 5, 2026 |website=Rolling Stone |language=en-US}}</ref> In 1981, the ''[[The Village Voice|Village Voice]]'' ranked "Call Me" as the third-best song of the year 1980 on its annual year-end critics' poll, [[Pazz & Jop]].<ref name="1980 poll">{{cite news |url=https://www.robertchristgau.com/xg/pnj/pjres80.php |title=The 1980 Pazz & Jop Critics Poll |work=[[The Village Voice]] |date=February 9, 1981 |access-date=February 13, 2013 |author=Christgau, Robert |author-link=Robert Christgau}}</ref> In 2017, ''Billboard'' ranked the song number three on their list of the 10 greatest Blondie songs,<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rock/blondie-10-best-songs-list-debbie-harry-7783994/|title=Blondie's 10 Greatest Songs: Critic's Picks|first=Ashley|last=Zlatopolsky|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=May 6, 2017|accessdate=April 6, 2022}}</ref> and in 2021, ''[[The Guardian]]'' ranked the song number four on their list of the 20 greatest Blondie songs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2021/oct/14/blondie-20-greatest-songs-ranked|title=Blondie's 20 greatest songs – ranked!|first=Alexis|last=Petridis|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=October 14, 2021|accessdate=April 6, 2022}}</ref>

